    <section class="section" id="about">
        <div class="container">
            <div class="about-editorial" data-anim="fade-up">
                <div class="about-editorial-left">
                    <div class="about-year-counter">
                        <span class="about-big-number" data-target="21">0</span>
                        <span class="about-big-label">iterations</span>
                    </div>
                    <div class="about-year-line"></div>
                    <p class="about-year-since">Est. 2005</p>
                </div>
                <div class="about-editorial-right">
                    <p class="section-eyebrow">About RBSMUN</p>
                    <h2
                        class="section-title"
                        style="text-align:left; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;"
                    >
                        Nepal's <span class="text-gradient">Pioneering</span><br
                        />Student-Run Conference
                    </h2>
                    <div class="about-editorial-body">
                        <p>
                            As one of the earliest Model United Nations
                            conferences in the country, RBSMUN has spent two
                            decades building and refining a space where students
                            become diplomats.
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            Hosted entirely by students of RBS, the conference
                            draws delegates from across Nepal to debate,
                            negotiate, and draft solutions to the world’s most
                            pressing challenges.
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            This year's theme — <em
                                >Unity &amp; Inclusion in a Fragmented World</em
                            > — asks students to confront an increasingly polarised
                            world head-on by exploring the structures that hold societies
                            together and those that tear them apart.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                    <div style="margin-top: 2.5rem;">
                        <a href="/about" class="btn btn-glass"
                            >Read our full history →</a
                        >
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section section-textured" id="letter">
        <div class="container">
            <div class="section-header" data-anim="reveal">
                <p class="section-eyebrow">A Message</p>
                <h2 class="section-title">
                    From the <span class="text-gradient">Secretary General</span
                    >
                </h2>
            </div>
            <div class="letter-layout" data-anim="morph-in">
                <!-- Flat card wrapper: no background panel or borders -->
                <div
                    class="letter-card-flat"
                    style="padding: 3rem 0; border: none; background: none; box-shadow: none;"
                >
                    <!-- Inner layout: Image floats to the right, letting text wrap below it -->
                    <div class="letter-inner">
                        <!-- SG photo floating on desktop -->
                        <div
                            class="letter-sg-photo"
                            style="float: right; width: clamp(280px, 35%, 400px); margin: 0 0 2rem 2.5rem; display: flex; flex-direction: column; align-items: center; gap: 1rem;"
                        >
                            <div
                                style="border-radius: 1rem; overflow: hidden; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.12); box-shadow: 0 8px 40px rgba(0,0,0,0.5);"
                            >
                                <img
                                    src={`${base}/images/team/secretariat/sg2.webp`}
                                    alt="Pragyan Gautam — Secretary General, RBSMUN 2026"
                                    style="width: 100%; display: block; object-fit: cover; aspect-ratio: 3/4;"
                                />
                            </div>
                            <div style="text-align: center;">
                                <p
                                    style="font-weight: 600; color: var(--text-primary); font-size: 0.95rem; margin: 0;"
                                >
                                    Pragyan Gautam
                                </p>
                                <p
                                    style="color: var(--text-secondary); font-size: 0.82rem; margin: 0.2rem 0 0;"
                                >
                                    Secretary-General
                                </p>
                                <p
                                    style="color: var(--accent-primary); font-size: 0.8rem; margin: 0.1rem 0 0;"
                                >
                                    RBSMUN 2026
                                </p>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        <!-- Letter text -->
                        <div class="letter-text-col">
                            <p class="letter-greeting">
                                Dearest Delegates and Faculty Advisors,
                            </p>
                            <p class="letter-text">
                                It is with immense pride and excitement that I
                                welcome you all to the twenty-first iteration of
                                the Rato Bangala School Model United Nations
                                Conference.
                            </p>
                            <p class="letter-text">
                                My name is Pragyan Gautam, a senior at Rato
                                Bangala School, and it is my privilege to serve
                                as the Secretary-General of RBSMUN XXI.
                            </p>
                            <p class="letter-text">
                                I did not begin debating because I dreamt of
                                changing the world. I started because I liked
                                winning. But as I attended international MUNS
                                and WSDC, I quickly realised there were rooms
                                where I was no longer the best speaker or the
                                best thinker. Surprisingly, that did not make me
                                enjoy MUNs any less – it made me love them even
                                more.
                            </p>
                            <p class="letter-text">
                                Over time, I realised that debate and Model
                                United Nations were never really about winning.
                                They were about growth. Every difficult
                                committee, every challenging POI, and every
                                disagreement pushed me a little further than I
                                thought I could go. And they will push you too.
                            </p>
                            <p class="letter-text">
                                I realise now that MUNs are also about
                                experiencing new perspectives and ideas from the
                                brightest of minds. Perhaps that is what makes
                                MUN so special. It gives young people like us
                                something we are not trusted with: a seat at the
                                table. For these three days, we get to become
                                diplomats, judges, legislators, journalists
                                discussing conflicts, global economy,
                                technologies, and human rights to name a few. We
                                are trusted to discuss these matters, not
                                because we have all the answers, but because we
                                are willing to search for them. In a world where
                                young people are often told to wait their turn,
                                MUN reminds us that our voices already matter.
                            </p>
                            <p class="letter-text">
                                So, if this is your first conference and you are
                                worried about forgetting your speech or
                                accidentally referring to yourself as "the
                                distinguished delegate" outside committee,
                                remember that everyone started somewhere. Growth
                                always disguises itself as discomfort. Whether
                                you leave RBSMUN with a medal or simply a copy
                                full of notes, I promise you will leave more
                                confident and much more capable than when you
                                arrived. But most importantly, remember to zoom
                                out a little and think about why we are doing
                                what we are doing. As future leaders of the
                                world, MUNs make us care about the world beyond
                                our borders and give us a sense of the impact we
                                are capable of making.
                            </p>
                            <p class="letter-text">
                                Since its inception in 2005, RBSMUN has remained
                                committed to fostering thoughtful dialogue,
                                principled diplomacy, and meaningful
                                collaboration. As one of Nepal's pioneering
                                student-run MUN conferences, it has become a
                                platform where delegates cultivate skills that
                                extend far beyond committee rooms – from
                                critical thinking and negotiation to empathy,
                                leadership, and cooperation.
                            </p>
                            <p class="letter-text">
                                Today, we live in a world that feels
                                increasingly fragmented. Wars continue to rage,
                                misinformation spreads faster than truth and
                                differences in identity, and ideology too often
                                become reasons for division rather than
                                dialogue. Yet history has repeatedly shown that
                                humanity has made its greatest progress not when
                                we have spoken the loudest, but when we have
                                listened the most.
                            </p>
                            <p class="letter-text">
                                I believe this year's theme, "Unity and
                                Inclusion in a Fragmented World," reflects that
                                belief. Unity does not mean thinking alike, nor
                                does inclusion require us to abandon our
                                differences. Instead, they remind us that
                                disagreement need not become hostility, and that
                                meaningful progress is only possible when every
                                voice has the opportunity to be heard.
                            </p>
                            <p class="letter-text">
                                This year's committee selection reflects that
                                spirit. Delegates will debate cyber warfare in
                                DISEC, sovereign debt reform in ECOFIN, and
                                questions of international law before the ICJ.
                                Others will navigate historical crises in the
                                Imperial Romanov Court and the Cambodian Crisis
                                Cabinet, address technology-facilitated violence
                                in UN Women, examine human rights in green
                                energy supply chains, and even deliberate over
                                divine authority in the Grand Convocation of the
                                Mythical Realms. Though every committee explores
                                a different challenge, they all ask the same
                                fundamental question: how do people with
                                competing interests build a shared future?
                            </p>
                            <p class="letter-text">
                                As delegates from across Nepal and beyond come
                                together, my hope is that RBSMUN XXI becomes
                                more than just another conference. I hope it
                                becomes a place where ideas are challenged
                                respectfully, friendships are built
                                unexpectedly, and perspectives are broadened
                                constantly. Long after the awards have been
                                handed out and the gavels packed away, I hope
                                you remember not just the resolutions you
                                drafted, but the conversations that changed how
                                you saw the world—and the confidence you
                                discovered in your own voice.
                            </p>
                            <p class="letter-text">
                                I look forward to welcoming you all this
                                December.
                            </p>
                            <div class="letter-signature">
                                <div class="signature-line"></div>
                                <p class="signature-name">Pragyan Gautam</p>
                                <p class="signature-title">
                                    The Secretary-General
                                </p>
                                <p class="signature-title">RBSMUN 2026</p>
                            </div>
                        </div><!-- end letter-text-col -->
                        <div style="clear: both;"></div>
                    </div><!-- end flat wrapper -->
                </div><!-- end letter-layout -->
            </div>
        </div>

        <section class="section cta-section" id="register">
            <div class="cta-glass-backdrop"></div>
            <div class="container">
                <div class="cta-content" data-anim="morph-in">
                    <div class="cta-glass-card glass-card">
